{"seq": 1, "type": "user_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T16:54:55.773644+00:00", "payload": {"text": "# Your workspace\n\nYou are in `/work`, inside a Linux container with no network access. Write a Go program here.\n[Maelstrom](https://github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom) will start that program once for every node\nof a simulated cluster, send it client requests, and check that the answers are correct.\n\nWhat is installed:\n\n- Go 1.27.\n- Maelstrom 0.2.4, as the `maelstrom` command, so you can test your program yourself.\n- Maelstrom's Go library, already in the module cache. Start your module with:\n\n      go mod init node\n      go get github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go@v0.0.0-20260710121306-480a8197026d\n\nA node talks to the rest of the cluster through standard input and output: it reads one JSON\nmessage per line on stdin and writes one per line on stdout. Each message has a `src`, a `dest`\nand a `body`, and the body's `type` says what it is. Anything written to stderr is a log line.\nThe library does that framing for you: `maelstrom.NewNode()`, then `Handle` a message type,\n`Reply` to a request, `Send` or `RPC` to another node, and `Run` to start. It also answers the\n`init` message that tells each node its own ID and the IDs of all the nodes.\n\n# Journals across three nodes\n\nWrite a node for a cluster of three that together keep many journals. A journal is an\nappend-only list of integer entries, named by a string, and each entry has a position in its\njournal. Readers also leave bookmarks saying how far they have read. Clients send any of four\nkinds of request to any of the three nodes, so all three must agree on every journal: the same\nentry at the same position, whichever node a client asks.\n\n`append` adds an entry to a journal and answers with its position. Positions in a journal may\nstart anywhere but must only increase, and no two entries in one journal share a position:\n\n    {\"type\": \"append\", \"msg_id\": 1, \"journal\": \"j7\", \"entry\": 55}\n    {\"type\": \"appended\", \"in_reply_to\": 1, \"position\": 30}\n\n`fetch` reads several journals at once, each from a given position. For each journal it answers\n`[position, entry]` pairs in position order, starting at that position or the first after it.\nIt may answer fewer pairs than the journal holds, and may leave out a journal with nothing at or\nafter the position asked for:\n\n    {\"type\": \"fetch\", \"msg_id\": 2, \"positions\": {\"j7\": 30, \"j9\": 0}}\n    {\"type\": \"fetched\", \"in_reply_to\": 2, \"entries\": {\"j7\": [[30, 55], [31, 8]], \"j9\": [[0, 4]]}}\n\n`bookmark` records, for each journal, the position a reader has finished reading up to:\n\n    {\"type\": \"bookmark\", \"msg_id\": 3, \"positions\": {\"j7\": 31}}\n    {\"type\": \"bookmarked\", \"in_reply_to\": 3}\n\n`bookmarks` asks for the bookmarked position of each journal named, leaving out journals with no\nbookmark:\n\n    {\"type\": \"bookmarks\", \"msg_id\": 4, \"journals\": [\"j7\", \"j9\"]}\n    {\"type\": \"bookmarks_are\", \"in_reply_to\": 4, \"positions\": {\"j7\": 31}}\n\nAn entry whose `append` was answered must never be lost or changed: a fetch that goes past its\nposition must not skip it, and every answer for that position must give the same entry. Nothing\nfails during the test.\n\nThe nodes can share a key/value store that Maelstrom runs as a service named `lin-kv`. It is\nlinearizable: a read always sees the latest write. In Go, `maelstrom.NewLinKV(node)` gives you\n`Read`, `ReadInt`, `Write` and `CompareAndSwap`, each taking a `context.Context`. Nodes may also\nmessage each other in any format you choose.\n\n## How it's graded\n\nYou have 45 minutes and 150 steps, a step being one reply from you, however many tools it runs. If either runs out, you are stopped where you are.\n\nWhen you stop, your program is built with `go build -o node .` and tested 3 times with:\n\n    MAELSTROM_RENAME_BIN=/work/node MAELSTROM_RENAME='{\"fields\": {\"key\": \"journal\", \"keys\": \"journals\", \"msg\": \"entry\", \"msgs\": \"entries\", \"offset\": \"position\", \"offsets\": \"positions\"}, \"types\": {\"commit_offsets\": \"bookmark\", \"commit_offsets_ok\": \"bookmarked\", \"list_committed_offsets\": \"bookmarks\", \"list_committed_offsets_ok\": \"bookmarks_are\", \"poll\": \"fetch\", \"poll_ok\": \"fetched\", \"send\": \"append\", \"send_ok\": \"appended\"}}' maelstrom test --workload kafka --bin /usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename --concurrency 2n --node-count 3 --rate 1000 --time-limit 20\n\nThe runs are independent, each with its own timing and faults. The attempt passes only if it builds and every run is valid.\n\nMaelstrom itself uses other names for these messages. `maelstrom-rename` stands in for your program, starts it, and translates every message between a client and a node, so test with the command above as it is.\n"}}
