es chunk transition but increases payload. 32 good.\nAppends need read then CAS; if conflict due other node, reread. Random tiny sleep to reduce synchronized contention. Go `math/rand` global concurrent safe. But no need.\nCAS create if missing with `from` empty vector: service ignores from. Race: two nodes both read missing and both CAS from [] to [entryA]/[entryB]. First creates, second sees key exists and compares from [] to current [A], conflict. Good.\nIf current vector is empty (could created but empty impossible), okay.\n\nFetch chunk:\n- read chunk. If missing, return empty.\n- If p negative? Positions likely >=0. User says positions may start anywhere but likely nonnegative. Could support negative by base0 index0. floor division in Go for negative truncates toward zero, bad. Clamp p&lt;0 to0.\n- If p maps to chunk index. Because positions start0 and contiguous.\n- Read chunk vector. index = p-base. If index &lt; len, return entries index:end. If index == len (possible p=base+len current, e.g p=32 but base0 if chunk size 32? Actually p=32 gives base32, so no). Only if chunkSize not dividing? divides.\n- If chunk missing at index, but maybe next chunk exists and p before base? No.\n- What if p=40 and chunk1 exists len5 (positions32-36), next chunk2 doesn't; empty. correct.\n- User says starting at p or first after it. If sparse due prior failures none. no failures.\n- Could fetch across chunks to return more than one. Returning only current chunk can cause issue at p=31: chunk0 returns [31] only (len32 index31 -&gt; one). client advances32. next poll chunk1. fine.\n- At p=0 returns 32. Fine.\n- If chunk size 32 and p=30 returns positions30,31. fine.\n- \"fetch that goes past its position must not skip it\" If requested position p points to an existing position, we include it. good.\n\nBookmarks:\nStore position per journal. Update should likely \"records ... position reader has finished reading up to\" and could decrease? Reader progress should not regress; actual commit offsets semantics says latest offsets, merge max. Use max.\nPer-journal key `__journal_bookmark__&lt;journal&gt;`. CAS:\n read value int; if missing, CAS create from 0 to desired? Service ignores from when missing. if desired maybe0. Could CAS `from`:0 `to`:desired.\n if old &gt;= desired no write.\n else CAS old-&gt;desired. On conflict loop.\nPotential user expects bookmark exact even lower? Wording records; Kafka says should advance and merge max. Use max.\nBookmarks query: read each key, omit missing. If value 0 exists, include 0. Read parallel.\n\nKey collisions: prefixes and journal arbitrary. `journal:&lt;raw&gt;:chunk:&lt;n&gt;` versus bookmark prefix distinct. A journal named weird cannot collide because raw inserted but suffix patterns could: journal \"x:chunk:1\" chunk key \"journal:x:chunk:1:chunk:0\", distinct from journal \"x\" key \"journal:x:chunk:1\". no collision because marker suffix and prefix. Journal could make chunk key equal another? Need injectivity of function f(j,c) = \"j:\"+j+\":c:\"+c. Could collide: j1=\"a:c:1\", c1=0 => j:a:c:1:c:0; j2=\"a\", c2? c is integer, cannot \"1:c:0\". no. Different c parse last marker; j can contain but last `:c:` ambiguous if j contains. e.g j1=\"x:c:1\", c1=2 -> \"j:x:c:1:c:2\"; j2=\"x\", c2=\"1:c:2\" not int. unique due final numeric.\nBookmark prefix `b:` vs chunk `j:`.\n\nMap request parsing:\n```\ntype appendBody struct { Journal string `json:\"journal\"`; Entry int `json:\"entry\"` }\n```\nJSON numbers decode int.\nfetch positions map[string]int; entries response map[string][][2]int. Go encoding `[2]int` -> `[[p,e],...]` yes.\nbookmark positions map[string]int.\nbookmarks journals []string response map string int.\nType response exact.\nNode.Reply automatically in_reply_to.\n\nError handling: return error -> library replies crash error. User says nothing fails. We should retry transient CAS. No context.\n\nNeed perhaps request messages to program have src \"c1\" and rename. n.Reply works.\