{"seq": 128, "type": "compaction", "ts": "2026-11-22T17:00:03.763357+00:00", "payload": {"replaced_count": 48, "summary": "Summary of the earlier conversation:\n## Task (from user)\n\nWrite a Go program in `/work` for a 3-node Maelstrom cluster (0.2.4) implementing shared journals. Node program must:\n- Handle four request types (as renamed by `maelstrom-rename`): `append`→reply `appended` with `position`; `fetch` (map journal→position) →reply `fetched` with `entries` map of `[[position, entry], ...]` in position order (may return fewer entries, may omit journals with nothing at/after the requested position); `bookmark` (map journal→position) →reply `bookmarked`; `bookmarks` (list of journals) →reply `bookmarks_are` with `positions` map (omit journals with no bookmark).\n- All 3 nodes must agree: same entry at same position on every node; positions only increase, unique per journal; an acknowledged append must never be lost/changed; a fetch must never skip a position it goes past (no gaps in returned pairs).\n- Clients hit any node; nodes may share the linearizable `lin-kv` service (`maelstrom.NewLinKV(node)` → `Read`, `ReadInt`, `Write`, `CompareAndSwap`, all taking `context.Context`).\n- Grading: `go build -o node .`, then 3 independent runs of:\n```\nMAELSTROM_RENAME_BIN=/work/node MAELSTROM_RENAME='{\"fields\": {\"key\": \"journal\", \"keys\": \"journals\", \"msg\": \"entry\", \"msgs\": \"entries\", \"offset\": \"position\", \"offsets\": \"positions\"}, \"types\": {\"commit_offsets\": \"bookmark\", \"commit_offsets_ok\": \"bookmarked\", \"list_committed_offsets\": \"bookmarks\", \"list_committed_offsets_ok\": \"bookmarks_are\", \"poll\": \"fetch\", \"poll_ok\": \"fetched\", \"send\": \"append\", \"send_ok\": \"appended\"}}' maelstrom test --workload kafka --bin /usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename --concurrency 2n --node-count 3 --rate 1000 --time-limit 20\n```\n- Note: rename only translates client↔node traffic; `lin-kv` service traffic still uses `read`/`write`/`cas`. 45 min / 150 steps budget.\n\n## Environment facts discovered\n\n- `/work` exists and is empty. Go 1.27.4. `maelstrom` at `/usr/local/bin/maelstrom`; `maelstrom-rename` at `/usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename`. Maelstrom source at `/opt/maelstrom` (docs, demos, `/opt/maelstrom/lib/maelstrom.jar`). `jar`/`unzip` NOT installed — use `python3 zipfile` to read the jar.\n- Module cache: `/go/pkg/mod/github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go@v0.0.0-20260710121306-480a8197026d` (contains `node.go`, `kv.go`, `rpc_error.go`). Module init command given by user: `go mod init node` and `go get github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go@v0.0.0-20260710121306-480a8197026d`.\n- Go KV API (from `/opt/maelstrom/demo/go/kv.go` grep): `NewLinKV(node *Node) *KV`; `CompareAndSwap(ctx, key string, from, to any, createIfNotExists bool) error`; also `Read`, `ReadInt`, `Write`. Exact signatures of `Read`/`Write`/`ReadInt` still need to be confirmed by reading `kv.go` fully.\n- `Node` API (from module `node.go`, partially read): `NewNode()`, `Init(id, nodeIDs)`, `ID()`, `NodeIDs()`, `Handle(typ, fn)` (panics on duplicate handler), `Run()`, plus `Reply`, `Send`, `RPC` (used in demos; exact signatures and whether handlers run concurrently still need confirming from full `node.go`).\n- lin-kv service (from `/opt/maelstrom/doc/services.md`): write, read, cas; `cas` supports `create_if_not_exists: true` for lazy init — very useful for journal metadata creation.\n\n## Reference implementations / extracted sources\n\n- `/opt/maelstrom/demo/clojure/kafka.clj`: multi-node Kafka demo storing log chunks in lin-kv: chunk key `log-<logkey>-<chunkIndex>`, chunk size 32, offsets under key `\"offsets\"`; useful pattern for chunked storage.\n- `/opt/maelstrom/demo/clojure/kafka_single_node.clj`: single-node in-memory Kafka demo.\n- Extracted from the jar to `/tmp`: `kafka.clj` (= `maelstrom/workload/kafka.clj`, 311 lines), `lin_kv.clj` (= `maelstrom/workload/lin_kv.clj`, 85 lines), `jepsen_tests_kafka.clj` (= `jepsen/tests/kafka.clj`, ~2577 lines: generator, `workload` at line 2537, checker).\n- Kafka checker anomalies (from `/tmp/jepsen_tests_kafka.clj` and workload docstring): **lost write** (client observes offset 10 but not offset 5 although 5 exists), **unobserved write** (existing offset never seen in any poll), **nonomonotonic** (offsets going backwards), internal vs external nonmonotonic; no recency requirement (may ack a send but not return it for a long time). The `workload` uses `:send`/`:poll` ops, string keys, and a final generator that polls all max offsets (so everything must eventually be observable).\n\n## Design thinking so far (not yet implemented)\n\n- Preferred: keep all journal state in `lin-kv` so all nodes trivially agree.\n  - Per-journal metadata key (e.g. `j/<journal>`) holding next position; claim position via CAS, then write entry at `e/<journal>/<pos>`; fetch scans from requested position upward while entries exist, stopping at first missing (guarantees contiguous, non-skipping responses; missing journals omitted).\n  - Caveats to handle: ordering between claiming a position and writing the entry (hole risk), concurrent appenders claiming the same position, and whether the metadata key format fits lin-kv value limits; avoid one giant JSON blob (workload runs ~1000 ops/s × 20 s ⇒ ~20k entries) — chunking or per-position keys preferred.\n  - Alternative if lin-kv is unavailable in the test run: node-to-node replication with a fixed leader (e.g., lowest node ID), forwarding appends, replicating before reply, ensuring followers never expose gaps.\n- Bookmarks: store per-journal position as max (monotonic) in lin-kv; `bookmarks` returns stored positions for named journals only.\n- Still must verify whether `lin-kv` is actually running during `maelstrom test` (check `maelstrom test --help` for a services flag / default services) — critical dependency of the design.\n\n## Remaining work\n\n1. Read full `/opt/maelstrom/demo/go/node.go` (handlers/concurrency, `Reply`/`Send`/`RPC` signatures) and `kv.go` (exact `Read`/`ReadInt`/`Write`/`CompareAndSwap` signatures, error behavior).\n2. Check `maelstrom test --help` (and docs) to confirm lin-kv service availability; if not available by default, adapt design.\n3. Skim the Kafka checker in `/tmp/jepsen_tests_kafka.clj` for exact anomaly rules (gap/lost-write detection, committed-offset checks) and the renamed field expectations.\n4. Create `/work/go.mod` (module `node`, require the maelstrom demo/go version) and `/work/main.go` implementing the renamed protocol (and ideally the original kafka names too for plain `maelstrom test` runs).\n5. Build (`go build -o node .`), test with the exact grading command, iterate until all 3 runs pass; verify no stdout pollution (only JSON lines) and logs to stderr only.\n\nThe user's current request, verbatim:\n# Your workspace\n\nYou are in `/work`, inside a Linux container with no network access. Write a Go program here.\n[Maelstrom](https://github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom) will start that program once for every node\nof a simulated cluster, send it client requests, and check that the answers are correct.\n\nWhat is installed:\n\n- Go 1.27.\n- Maelstrom 0.2.4, as the `maelstrom` command, so you can test your program yourself.\n- Maelstrom's Go library, already in the module cache. Start your module with:\n\n      go mod init node\n      go get github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go@v0.0.0-20260710121306-480a8197026d\n\nA node talks to the rest of the cluster through standard input and output: it reads one JSON\nmessage per line on stdin and writes one per line on stdout. Each message has a `src`, a `dest`\nand a `body`, and the body's `type` says what it is. Anything written to stderr is a log line.\nThe library does that framing for you: `maelstrom.NewNode()`, then `Handle` a message type,\n`Reply` to a request, `Send` or `RPC` to another node, and `Run` to start. It also answers the\n`init` message that tells each node its own ID and the IDs of all the nodes.\n\n# Journals across three nodes\n\nWrite a node for a cluster of three that together keep many journals. A journal is an\nappend-only list of integer entries, named by a string, and each entry has a position in its\njournal. Readers also leave bookmarks saying how far they have read. Clients send any of four\nkinds of request to any of the three nodes, so all three must agree on every journal: the same\nentry at the same position, whichever node a client asks.\n\n`append` adds an entry to a journal and answers with its position. Positions in a journal may\nstart anywhere but must only increase, and no two entries in one journal share a position:\n\n    {\"type\": \"append\", \"msg_id\": 1, \"journal\": \"j7\", \"entry\": 55}\n    {\"type\": \"appended\", \"in_reply_to\": 1, \"position\": 30}\n\n`fetch` reads several journals at once, each from a given position. For each journal it answers\n`[position, entry]` pairs in position order, starting at that position or the first after it.\nIt may answer fewer pairs than the journal holds, and may leave out a journal with nothing at or\nafter the position asked for:\n\n    {\"type\": \"fetch\", \"msg_id\": 2, \"positions\": {\"j7\": 30, \"j9\": 0}}\n    {\"type\": \"fetched\", \"in_reply_to\": 2, \"entries\": {\"j7\": [[30, 55], [31, 8]], \"j9\": [[0, 4]]}}\n\n`bookmark` records, for each journal, the position a reader has finished reading up to:\n\n    {\"type\": \"bookmark\", \"msg_id\": 3, \"positions\": {\"j7\": 31}}\n    {\"type\": \"bookmarked\", \"in_reply_to\": 3}\n\n`bookmarks` asks for the bookmarked position of each journal named, leaving out journals with no\nbookmark:\n\n    {\"type\": \"bookmarks\", \"msg_id\": 4, \"journals\": [\"j7\", \"j9\"]}\n    {\"type\": \"bookmarks_are\", \"in_reply_to\": 4, \"positions\": {\"j7\": 31}}\n\nAn entry whose `append` was answered must never be lost or changed: a fetch that goes past its\nposition must not skip it, and every answer for that position must give the same entry. Nothing\nfails during the test.\n\nThe nodes can share a key/value store that Maelstrom runs as a service named `lin-kv`. It is\nlinearizable: a read always sees the latest write. In Go, `maelstrom.NewLinKV(node)` gives you\n`Read`, `ReadInt`, `Write` and `CompareAndSwap`, each taking a `context.Context`. Nodes may also\nmessage each other in any format you choose.\n\n## How it's graded\n\nYou have 45 minutes and 150 steps, a step being one reply from you, however many tools it runs. If either runs out, you are stopped where you are.\n\nWhen you stop, your program is built with `go build -o node .` and tested 3 times with:\n\n    MAELSTROM_RENAME_BIN=/work/node MAELSTROM_RENAME='{\"fields\": {\"key\": \"journal\", \"keys\": \"journals\", \"msg\": \"entry\", \"msgs\": \"entries\", \"offset\": \"position\", \"offsets\": \"positions\"}, \"types\": {\"commit_offsets\": \"bookmark\", \"commit_offsets_ok\": \"bookmarked\", \"list_committed_offsets\": \"bookmarks\", \"list_committed_offsets_ok\": \"bookmarks_are\", \"poll\": \"fetch\", \"poll_ok\": \"fetched\", \"send\": \"append\", \"send_ok\": \"appended\"}}' maelstrom test --workload kafka --bin /usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename --concurrency 2n --node-count 3 --rate 1000 --time-limit 20\n\nThe runs are independent, each with its own timing and faults. The attempt passes only if it builds and every run is valid.\n\nMaelstrom itself uses other names for these messages. `maelstrom-rename` stands in for your program, starts it, and translates every message between a client and a node, so test with the command above as it is.\n", "usage": {"input_tokens": 15503, "output_tokens": 6145}}}
