ns that might be lower than the current bookmark (out-of-order concurrent requests from different clients). Using max means the response acknowledges the lower value but stores higher. Checker might require the stored value equals the requested value? Real Kafka semantics are max. The user text says \"records ... the position a reader has finished reading up to\" — a lower value would be a regression. The Kafka checker's `check-committed-offsets`? Let's grep the checker for commit offsets validation to be safe.", "text": "", "tool_calls": [{"arguments": {"command": "grep -n \"commit\" /tmp/jepsen_tests_kafka.clj | head -60", "timeout_seconds": 20}, "arguments_error": null, "id": "call_01a2043365f875e38d751ced", "name": "shell"}], "usage": {"input_tokens": 48921, "output_tokens": 593}}}
