nk` unmarshal: `body.Value` is `json.RawMessage`; the read_ok body is `{\"type\":\"read_ok\",\"in_reply_to\":N,\"value\":[...]}`. Our struct with `Value json.RawMessage` — since the raw message of \"value\" is `[1,2]`, unmarshal into []int works ✓.\n\nFor bookmark: `Value int` — the value is a number ✓.\n\nEverything is fine.\n\nOne more consideration: performance at higher rates. The grading uses rate 1000 with 3 nodes and 6 workers; we handled ~7100 sends in 20s = 355/s (limited by workload key caps: 512 keys × 16 = 8192 max sends; we did 7100). Not rate-limited by us. Good.\n\nNow let me think about whether the checker \"unobserved write\" might fail due to our fetch only returning up to 32 entries per poll and the workload's final generator clipping to 10 seconds. With ≤16 entries per key, fine.\n\nI think the implementation is solid. Let me do a final cleanup:\n1. Remove the `store` directory (test artifacts).\n2. Simplify the code slightly (remove the awkward contextBackground helper? It's fine but a bit odd). Let me clean it up: use context.Background() directly and remove the helper. Also remove the unused `readChunk` bool return? It's used... `entries, _, err`. The bool isn't used anywhere. Simplify to return ([]int, error). But the bool distinguishes missing vs empty — not needed. Let me simplify for clarity.\n\nAlso, one improvement for robustness: in `append`, use a local variable `next` derived from the chunk index rather than a persistent variable, to avoid stale state. Actually current code is fine but let me restructure:\n\n```go\nfor {\n    chunk := 0\n    for {\n        entries, err := readChunk(journal, chunk)\n        if len(entries) < chunkSize {\n            ok := writeChunk(...)\n            if ok { return chunk*chunkSize+len(entries) }\n            break // re-read same chunk\n        }\n        chunk++\n    }\n}\n```\nThis is cleaner. Let me rewrite that function.\n\nAlso potential improvement: use `chunkSize = 64`? No need.\n\nLet me also add handling for the original kafka protocol? I'll skip it to minimize risk... Hmm, but what if the grader also runs a plain test? The user explicitly gave the grading command with rename. The task statement says \"Handle four request types (as renamed by maelstrom-rename)\". So only renamed names. Skip.\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider one thing: the user says \"Nodes may share the linearizable lin-kv service\". This is a strong hint that the intended solution uses lin-kv. ✓.\n\nLet me finalize the code cleanup and rebuild, then run a shorter sanity test (maybe 5s again) to ensure the refactor didn't break anything. I have plenty of steps left (used ~15). Let's do it carefully.\n\nAlso: should I handle the case where `lin-kv` is unavailable? \"The nodes can share a key/value store that Maelstrom runs as a service named lin-kv.\" It's always there by default (default-services). ✓\n\nOne more consideration about robustness: if a KV read returns an error other than key-not-exist (e.g., timeout), our handler returns an error → crash reply to the client → the checker may flag \"bad-error-types\". Currently no timeouts happen. To be extra safe, I could retry reads a few times on transient errors. The service never fails, and node processes aren't killed, so no transient errors. But a defensive retry loop is cheap insurance. Let me add a helper `kvRPC` that retries on error (except KeyDoesNotExist/PreconditionFailed) up to N times with small backoff.\n\nCareful: retrying a CAS that actually succeeded but whose response was lost could double-append. But responses aren't lost (no network faults). I'll only retry on *read* errors, and for CAS, retry only if the error is not PreconditionFailed/KeyDoesNotExist... Hmm, if a CAS times out after succeeding, retrying with the same \"from\" would fail with PreconditionFailed (since the value changed) — and then we'd re-read and append the entry again, causing a duplicate entry (the same append acknowledged twice → positions differ). To be safe, on CAS error (non-precondition), we should re-read and check whether our entry is already there... but entries can legitimately repeat values, so we can't tell.\n\nGiven no failures occur, I'll keep it simple: no retries on CAS beyond the precondition loop (which is correct and safe). For reads, a retry is safe. I'll add a small retry for reads only (3 attempts).\n\nActually, the KV service never fails, so even that is unnecessary. But a read retry is harmless. I'll add it.\n\nLet me now rewrite main.go cleanly.", "text": "The exact grading command passed (`:valid? true`). Now let me clean up the implementation slightly and re-verify.", "tool_calls": [{"arguments": {"content": "// A Maelstrom node that implements shared journals.\n//\n// Every node keeps its journal state in Maelstrom's linearizable \"lin-kv\"\n// service, so all three nodes observe exactly the same log: the same entry at\n// the same position, no matter which node a client talks to.\n//\n// A journal is stored as a sequence of fixed-size chunks.  A chunk holds the\n// entries of one contiguous range of positions and is only ever extended, so\n// allocating a position and storing its entry is a single atomic\n// compare-and-swap: no position can be skipped, duplicated, or lost.\npackage main\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"log\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"strconv\"\n\t\"sync\"\n\t\"time\"\n\n\tmaelstrom \"github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go\"\n)\n\nconst (\n\t// chunkSize is the number of entries in one chunk of a journal.\n\tchunkSize = 32\n\n\t// kvService is Maelstrom's linearizable key/value service.\n\tkvService = \"lin-kv\"\n\n\t// readAttempts bounds retries of a read against the KV service.\n\treadAttempts = 5\n)\n\n// The four client request bodies, after maelstrom-rename has translated them.\ntype appendRequest struct {\n\tJournal string `json:\"journal\"`\n\tEntry   int    `json:\"entry\"`\n}\n\ntype fetchRequest struct {\n\tPositions map[string]int `json:\"positions\"`\n}\n\ntype bookmarkRequest struct {\n\tPositions map[string]int `json:\"positions\"`\n}\n\ntype bookmarksRequest struct {\n\tJournals []string `json:\"journals\"`\n}\n\n// server implements the journal operations on top of lin-kv.\ntype server struct {\n\tnode *maelstrom.Node\n\n\t// Updates to one journal are serialized within a process, which keeps\n\t// compare-and-swap conflicts to (at most) one per other node.\n\tlocksMu sync.Mutex\n\tlocks   map[string]*sync.Mutex\n}\n\nfunc newServer(node *maelstrom.Node) *server {\n\treturn &server{\n\t\tnode:  node,\n\t\tlocks: make(map[string]*sync.Mutex),\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc (s *server) lockFor(name string) *sync.Mutex {\n\ts.locksMu.Lock()\n\tdefer s.locksMu.Unlock()\n\tlock := s.locks[name]\n\tif lock == nil {\n\t\tlock = new(sync.Mutex)\n\t\ts.locks[name] = lock\n\t}\n\treturn lock\n}\n\n// chunkKey is the KV key holding one chunk of a journal.\nfunc chunkKey(journal string, chunk int) string {\n\treturn \"journals:\" + journal + \":chunks:\" + strconv.Itoa(chunk)\n}\n\n// bookmarkKey is the KV key holding a journal's bookmark.\nfunc bookmarkKey(journal string) string {\n\treturn \"journal-bookmarks:\" + journal\n}\n\n// readChunk returns the entries stored in one chunk of a journal.  A chunk\n// that does not exist yet reads as an empty chunk.\nfunc (s *server) readChunk(journal string, chunk int) ([]int, error) {\n\tkey := chunkKey(journal, chunk)\n\tvar lastErr error\n\tfor attempt := 0; attempt < readAttempts; attempt++ {\n\t\tmsg, err := s.node.SyncRPC(context.Background(), kvService, map[string]any{\n\t\t\t\"type\": \"read\",\n\t\t\t\"key\":  key,\n\t\t})\n\t\tif err == nil {\n\t\t\tvar body struct {\n\t\t\t\tValue []int `json:\"value\"`\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(msg.Body, &body); err != nil {\n\t\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"decode chunk %q: %w\", journal, err)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn body.Value, nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tif maelstrom.ErrorCode(err) == maelstrom.KeyDoesNotExist {\n\t\t\treturn nil, nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tlastErr = err\n\t\ttime.Sleep(time.Duration(attempt+1) * time.Millisecond)\n\t}\n\treturn nil, lastErr\n}\n\n// extendChunk atomically appends an entry to a chunk.  It reports whether the\n// compare-and-swap succeeded; a false result means another writer changed the\n// chunk first and the caller must re-read it.\nfunc (s *server) extendChunk(journal string, chunk int, old []int, entry int) (bool, error) {\n\tnext := make([]int, len(old)+1)\n\tcopy(next, old)\n\tnext[len(old)] = entry\n\n\t_, err := s.node.SyncRPC(context.Background(), kvService, map[string]any{\n\t\t\"type\":                 \"cas\",\n\t\t\"key\":                  chunkKey(journal, chunk),\n\t\t\"from\":                 old,\n\t\t\"to\":                   next,\n\t\t\"create_if_not_exists\": true,\n\t})\n\tif err == nil {\n\t\treturn true, nil\n\t}\n\tif maelstrom.ErrorCode(err) == maelstrom.PreconditionFailed {\n\t\treturn false, nil\n\t}\n\treturn false, err\n}\n\n// append adds an entry to a journal and returns its position.\nfunc (s *server) append(journal string, entry int) (int, error) {\n\tlock := s.lockFor(\"journal:\" + journal)\n\tlock.Lock()\n\tdefer lock.Unlock()\n\n\tfor {\n\t\tfor chunk := 0; ; chunk++ {\n\t\t\tentries, err := s.readChunk(journal, chunk)\n\t\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\t\treturn 0, err\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif len(entries) == chunkSize {\n\t\t\t\t// This chunk is full; the next position lives in the next one.\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\n\t\t\tok, err := s.extendChunk(journal, chunk, entries, entry)\n\t\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\t\treturn 0, err\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif ok {\n\t\t\t\treturn chunk*chunkSize + len(entries), nil\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t// Somebody else changed this chunk.  Re-read it and try again;\n\t\t\t// the entries we read are never lost or overwritten.\n\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\n// fetch returns the entries of a journal at or after the requested position.\n// The returned pairs are contiguous and in increasing position order.\nfunc (s *server) fetch(journal string, position int) ([][2]int, error) {\n\tif position < 0 {\n\t\tposition = 0\n\t}\n\tchunk := position / chunkSize\n\tbase := chunk * chunkSize\n\n\tentries, err := s.readChunk(journal, chunk)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\tindex := position - base\n\tif index < 0 {\n\t\tindex = 0\n\t}\n\tif index >= len(entries) {\n\t\treturn nil, nil\n\t}\n\n\tresult := make([][2]int, 0, len(entries)-index)\n\tfor i := index; i < len(entries); i++ {\n\t\tresult = append(result, [2]int{base + i, entries[i]})\n\t}\n\treturn result, nil\n}\n\n// advanceBookmark moves a journal's bookmark forward.  Bookmarks never move\n// backwards, so concurrent readers cannot undo one another's progress.\nfunc (s *server) advanceBookmark(journal string, position int) error {\n\tlock := s.lockFor(\"bookmark:\" + journal)\n\tlock.Lock()\n\tdefer lock.Unlock()\n\n\tfor {\n\t\tmsg, err := s.node.SyncRPC(context.Background(), kvService, map[string]any{\n\t\t\t\"type\": \"read\",\n\t\t\t\"key\":  bookmarkKey(journal),\n\t\t})\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tif maelstrom.ErrorCode(err) != maelstrom.KeyDoesNotExist {\n\t\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t// The service ignores \"from\" when creating a missing key.\n\t\t\t_, err = s.node.SyncRPC(context.Background(), kvService, map[string]any{\n\t\t\t\t\"type\":                 \"cas\",\n\t\t\t\t\"key\":                  bookmarkKey(journal),\n\t\t\t\t\"from\":                 0,\n\t\t\t\t\"to\":                   position,\n\t\t\t\t\"create_if_not_exists\": true,\n\t\t\t})\n\t\t\tif err == nil {\n\t\t\t\treturn nil\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif maelstrom.ErrorCode(err) != maelstrom.PreconditionFailed {\n\t\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t// Another node created the bookmark first; re-read it.\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\n\t\tvar body struct {\n\t\t\tValue int `json:\"value\"`\n\t\t}\n\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(msg.Body, &body); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\t\tif body.Value >= position {\n\t\t\treturn nil\n\t\t}\n\n\t\t_, err = s.node.SyncRPC(context.Background(), kvService, map[string]any{\n\t\t\t\"type\": \"cas\",\n\t\t\t\"key\":  bookmarkKey(journal),\n\t\t\t\"from\": body.Value,\n\t\t\t\"to\":   position,\n\t\t})\n\t\tif err == nil {\n\t\t\treturn nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tif maelstrom.ErrorCode(err) != maelstrom.PreconditionFailed {\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\t\t// Another node moved the bookmark; re-read it.\n\t}\n}\n\n// readBookmark returns a journal's bookmark, if it has one.\nfunc (s *server) readBookmark(journal string) (int, bool, error) {\n\tmsg, err := s.node.SyncRPC(context.Background(), kvService, map[string]any{\n\t\t\"type\": \"read\",\n\t\t\"key\":  bookmarkKey(journal),\n\t})\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tif maelstrom.ErrorCode(err) == maelstrom.KeyDoesNotExist {\n\t\t\treturn 0, false, nil\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn 0, false, err\n\t}\n\tvar body struct {\n\t\tValue int `json:\"value\"`\n\t}\n\tif err := json.Unmarshal(msg.Body, &body); err != nil {\n\t\treturn 0, false, err\n\t}\n\treturn body.Value, true, nil\n}\n\nfunc (s *server) handleAppend(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n\tvar body appendRequest\n\tif err := json.Unmarshal(msg.Body, &body); err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tposition, err := s.append(body.Journal, body.Entry)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\treturn s.node.Reply(msg, map[string]any{\n\t\t\"type\":     \"appended\",\n\t\t\"position\": position,\n\t})\n}\n\nfunc (s *server) handleFetch(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n\tvar body fetchRequest\n\tif err := json.Unmarshal(msg.Body, &body); err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\n\ttype result struct {\n\t\tjournal string\n\t\tentries [][2]int\n\t\terr     error\n\t}\n\tresults := make(chan result, len(body.Positions))\n\tfor journal, position := range body.Positions {\n\t\tgo func(journal string, position int) {\n\t\t\tentries, err := s.fetch(journal, position)\n\t\t\tresults <- result{journal: journal, entries: entries, err: err}\n\t\t}(journal, position)\n\t}\n\n\tentries := make(map[string][][2]int, len(body.Positions))\n\tfor range body.Positions {\n\t\tr := <-results\n\t\tif r.err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn r.err\n\t\t}\n\t\tif len(r.entries) > 0 {\n\t\t\tentries[r.journal] = r.entries\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn s.node.Reply(msg, map[string]any{\n\t\t\"type\":    \"fetched\",\n\t\t\"entries\": entries,\n\t})\n}\n\nfunc (s *server) handleBookmark(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n\tvar body bookmarkRequest\n\tif err := json.Unmarshal(msg.Body, &body); err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\n\ttype update struct {\n\t\terr error\n\t}\n\tupdates := make(chan update, len(body.Positions))\n\tfor journal, position := range body.Positions {\n\t\tgo func(journal string, position int) {\n\t\t\tupdates <- update{err: s.advanceBookmark(journal, position)}\n\t\t}(journal, position)\n\t}\n\tfor range body.Positions {\n\t\tu := <-updates\n\t\tif u.err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn u.err\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn s.node.Reply(msg, map[string]any{\"type\": \"bookmarked\"})\n}\n\nfunc (s *server) handleBookmarks(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n\tvar body bookmarksRequest\n\tif err := json.Unmarshal(msg.Body, &body); err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\n\ttype result struct {\n\t\tjournal  string\n\t\tposition int\n\t\tpresent  bool\n\t\terr      error\n\t}\n\tresults := make(chan result, len(body.Journals))\n\tfor _, journal := range body.Journals {\n\t\tgo func(journal string) {\n\t\t\tposition, present, err := s.readBookmark(journal)\n\t\t\tresults <- result{journal: journal, position: position, present: present, err: err}\n\t\t}(journal)\n\t}\n\n\tpositions := make(map[string]int, len(body.Journals))\n\tfor range body.Journals {\n\t\tr := <-results\n\t\tif r.err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn r.err\n\t\t}\n\t\tif r.present {\n\t\t\tpositions[r.journal] = r.position\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn s.node.Reply(msg, map[string]any{\n\t\t\"type\":      \"bookmarks_are\",\n\t\t\"positions\": positions,\n\t})\n}\n\nfunc main() {\n\t// The library logs every message it sends and receives.  At the\n\t// benchmark's request rate that is expensive and noisy, so discard those\n\t// logs; real failures are still reported on stderr.\n\tlog.SetOutput(io.Discard)\n\n\tn := maelstrom.NewNode()\n\ts := newServer(n)\n\tn.Handle(\"append\", s.handleAppend)\n\tn.Handle(\"fetch\", s.handleFetch)\n\tn.Handle(\"bookmark\", s.handleBookmark)\n\tn.Handle(\"bookmarks\", s.handleBookmarks)\n\n\tif err := n.Run(); err != nil {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"node failed: %v\\n\", err)\n\t\tos.Exit(1)\n\t}\n}\n", "path": "/work/main.go"}, "arguments_error": null, "id": "call_01a2043693057943b68bcd1b", "name": "write_file"}], "usage": {"input_tokens": 59399, "output_tokens": 5919}}}
